## Context

`docs/jetp-fusion.md` (ticket 1701, author decisions 2026-09-30) changes the
model of a matching judgement; the storage contract § 1 and § 4, the DDL, the
in-force views and the builders still implement the previous one.

Author decisions (2026-09-30, interactive):

1. "A pending revision does not erase an accepted decision." The storage rule
   (the terminal row decides, so a terminal `candidate` removes the accepted
   row it supersedes) is replaced by the fusion rule. Measured 2026-09-30: 0
   candidate rows supersede an accepted row across `line-referents`,
   `party-names` and `relations.d` (about 4,600 rows), so no published figure
   moves today.
2. No threshold at write time. The author asked "Why accept, not list with
   dynamic cutoff?": each judgement keeps its stance, and each result declares
   the cutoff a candidate match needs in order to count.
3. Likelihood and confidence use the IPCC calibrated scales (Mastrandrea et
   al. 2010), not a single number in [0, 1]: "NP, DST, LUP ... are all the
   same thing ... we are NOT doing automatic fusion, we are doing LLM-based
   smart fusion. Refer to the qualitative IPCC judgement scale."
4. Analyst-defined counting scopes leave the ontology's `perimeters`: the
   World Bank reference pool in `data/jetp/ontology/perimeters.csv` is one.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The in-force rule keeps an accepted decision in force while a later revision is only proposed; a red test replays a candidate superseding an accepted row
- [ ] A matching judgement records a stance, an IPCC likelihood term and an IPCC confidence level; the `matching.panel` rule emits them; existing `confidence` values are mapped by a stated, reviewed rule
- [ ] Every derived result declares its matching cutoff in its run record, and the observatory's counts are produced at that cutoff; figures at the current default are unchanged or each change is explained
- [ ] Counting scopes are stored apart from publisher-stated perimeters, and nothing in the build reads the World Bank pool as a perimeter
- [ ] The storage contract § 1 and § 4 describe what the code does
- [ ] A statement (line) records the method, method version and reviewer (checking reader, and the author where they decided) that read it, as `docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 3 requires
- [ ] The statement identifier scheme says how ordinals continue across snapshots of one document, so a new snapshot's statements get new identifiers without collision (`docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 9)
- [ ] Proposed schema changes from the vocabulary pass (ticket 1703) are decided and, where accepted, migrated: the D1 `coverage` table, which records the search per listed referent, is renamed to say search (for example `referent-searches`), and the round log and unreachable list of `docs/jetp-collection.md` § 5 and § 8 get tables of their own; the authority category `secondary_source` is renamed so that the retired word *source* leaves the schema; the retrieval statuses `not_published` and `not_applicable`, which are verdicts rather than fetch outcomes, are related to collection's terminal verdicts; the report's proposed rename of the row status `candidate` to `proposed` is decided (its verifier advised against it)
- [ ] `line-referents` and `relations` carry `recorded_at` (ledger write time) beside `decided_at`, and a DDL test replays the as-of case of the storage contract § 1: A accepted, B superseding A recorded after K, the state at K returns A (review wave 1, W1-06)
- [ ] `line-referents` and `relations` carry `basis` (the quoted basis) beside stance, likelihood and confidence, and `adjudications` carries `method`, `method_version`, stance, likelihood, confidence, basis and a justification (W1-08)
- [ ] `line-referents.justification_line_ids` is replaced by rows of a relation table, one per decision row and justification line (W1-08)
- [ ] `line-field-specs` is keyed by document and table segment and maps each printed header, with its unit and scale wording, to a field name under a versioned mapping (W1-13)
- [ ] `lines.classification` is NOT NULL in the DDL (W1-40)
- [ ] `documents.edition_of` is dropped; the `relations` row is the one home of an edition relation (W1-44)
- [ ] The writer splits an oversized `line-fields/<document_id>` into numbered `.d/NN.csv` shards, and a living document's fields table by year of `recorded_at` (W1-14, W1-53)
- [ ] The validator checks prose locators as start and end anchors derived from the verbatim quote (extraction § 5) on lines admitted after the rule, and accepts `text` as the table segment of a prose line identifier (W1-02, W1-09)
- [ ] A `document-addresses` table records a document's addresses with the date from which each holds, and the validator counts a retrieval of any recorded address as a retrieval of the document [M4] (W1-51)
